tacked bishop to safety.","The next best move, 6.Nxg5, would drop White's winning chances from 52% to 24% (−3.20).","6.Bg3 gets the bishop out of the way of …gxh4.","Black was threatening …gxh4, winning a bishop. The bishop steps away.","Black answers 6…d6, taking e5 away from the knight on f3 and the bishop on g3.","Engine line: 6…d6 7.Nc3 Nh5 8.e3 Bg7 9.Bd3 (+0.18)."]},{"ply":61,"name":"31.bxc6","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"31.bxc6 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 50% to 21%.","points":["Black answers 31…d4: a pawn break: it challenges the e3 pawn to open lines.","After d4, Black wins 2 points of material within 8 moves.","Better was 31.Rb4 (0.00)."],"better":"31.Rb4 backs up the pawn lever c6 to pry open the b-file. After 31…Ra8 32.bxc6 Bxc6 33.Bb5 Ra3 34.Bxc6 bxc6 35.Rb6, the bishops are traded (0.00)."},{"ply":62,"name":"31…Bxc6","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"31…Bxc6 misses the chance 31.bxc6 gave: 31…d4 would have punished it. Black's winning chances go from 79% to 68%.","points":["White answers 32.Rb6, which pins the bishop on c6 to the king: it can't move at all.","Better was 31…d4 (−3.58): a pawn break: it challenges the e3 pawn to open lines."],"better":"31…d4 wins two pawns, picking it up with 32…Bxc6, and stops cxd7. After 32.Ke2 Bxc6 33.Rb4 Rxe3+ 34.Kd2 Bxg2 35.Rxd4 Rge8, Black comes out two pawns up (−3.58)."},{"ply":63,"name":"32.Bb5","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"32.Bb5 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 32% to 11%.","points":["Black answers 32…d4+: moving away uncovers check from the bishop on c6.","After d4+, Black wins 1 point of material within 4 moves.","Better was 32.Rb6 (−2.03), which pins the bishop on c6 to the king: it can't move at all."],"better":"32.Rb6 pins the bishop on c6 to the king. After 32…d4+ 33.Rxc6+ bxc6 34.exd4 Rd8 35.Bc4, White is a pawn down (−2.03)."},{"ply":64,"name":"32…d4+","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"32…d4+! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moving away uncovers check from the bishop on c6.","A pawn break: it challenges the e3 pawn to open lines.","The next best move, 32…Re4, would drop Black's winning chances from 89% to 47% (+0.28).","32…d4+ wins two pawns, picking it up with 33…Rxe3+.","Sets up a sequence and wins two pawns once the captures settle, picking it up with 33…Rxe3+.","White answers 33.Ke2, leaving Black with an isolated pawn on d4."]},{"ply":93,"name":"47.Kd3","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"47.Kd3 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 3% to 0%. It allows mate in 4.","points":["Black answers 47…Ke5, which protects the d4 pawn, which was hanging.","Better was 47.Ke1 (−9.74), which attacks the rook on f2, which is undefended."],"better":"47.Ke1 threatens Kxf2, winning a rook. After 47…Rb2 48.Be2 Re5 49.Kf2 Rexe2+ 50.Kg3, White is a piece down (−9.74)."}],"opening":{"name":"Indian Defense: Dzindzi-Indian Defense","eco":"E10","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 4.Bg5: 7 moves in all, 4 by White and 3 by Black.","Black left the book with 4…h6. In 32 games from this position, strong players played 4…d5 (34%), 4…c5 (25%), 4…b5 (19%) or 4…Be7 (9%).","The main line goes on 4…d5 5.e3 dxc4 6.Bxc4.","From here White scored 59% in those games, with 11% drawn."]}}